    "content": "## This is the trip to photograph\n\nFamilies photograph beach holidays and skip the one trip that matters most: the one with the grandparents. Three generations in one frame is the photograph that gains value every year — and it needs planning that a couples session never does, because the group spans someone who naps at two and someone who won't climb stairs.\n\n## The route rules\n\n**Flat, shaded, benched.** The route must respect the oldest knees and the youngest attention span at once. Spain's gift is that its best backdrops often qualify: Seville's Plaza de España is flat with constant colonnade shade and benches every twenty metres; Madrid's Retiro rolls gently with the glass palace as a centrepiece; Barcelona's Ciutadella park does the same with the cascada. What disqualifies itself: Granada's Albaicín (stairs), coastal cliff paths, anything cobbled and steep at once.\n\n**One location, not three.** A three-generation session moves at the pace of its slowest member times its most distractible one. Sixty to ninety minutes in ONE well-chosen place beats a transfer-heavy itinerary every time.\n\n**Morning, always.** Grandparents are fresher, children are fresher, the light is soft and the heat — which hits older people hardest — hasn't arrived. In a Seville June, a 9am start is not a preference; it is the plan.\n\n## The shot list that matters\n\nTell the photographer these five in advance, because they don't happen by accident:\n\n1. **The full group**, seated and relaxed — benches beat standing lines.\n2. **Grandparents with each grandchild alone.** The frame nobody has at home.\n3. **The hands.** Three generations of hands together: the close-up that becomes the print.\n4. **The walk from behind** — grandfather and grandson, unposed.\n5. **The laugh you cannot direct** — which is why the photographer shoots the in-between moments while everyone thinks the photo is over.\n\n## Pacing for the two fragile ends\n\nPlan the session as three acts of twenty minutes with sitting breaks between: group work first while everyone is fresh, then grandparents-and-grandchildren while parents wrangle snacks, then the play at the end when small children are done obeying. A photographer used to [family sessions](/photoshoots/family) runs this structure without being asked — it is worth asking anyway.\n\n## Where in Spain, by season\n\nOctober to May: **Seville** — warm, flat, golden. June to September: **the north** — San Sebastián's promenade or Santiago's old town keep three generations comfortable while the south bakes. Year-round: **Madrid**'s Retiro, two flights from everywhere and benches all the way. For islands with the whole clan, Mallorca's Palma old town beats the coves — cathedral backdrop, level streets, ice cream within reach.\n"
