    "content": "## What an hour really holds\n\nSixty minutes of photography holds: one area on foot, two or three micro-locations inside it, one outfit (a second if the change is fast), and 40–60 delivered photos. That is genuinely enough for most travel sessions — a couple in Seville's Santa Cruz, a solo portrait walk through Madrid's old centre, a family in one park.\n\nWhat an hour does not hold: transfers. The minute a car or metro enters the plan, the hour is no longer an hour of photography.\n\n## When the second hour pays for itself\n\n- **Two distinct worlds.** Barcelona's Gothic lanes AND the beach. Madrid's Retiro AND Gran Vía. If the gallery should look like two different trips, the session needs the travel time inside it.\n- **Groups above four.** Every person added is minutes of coordination; a family of six in one hour produces group shots and nothing else. The second hour is where the individual combinations live — grandparents with each child, the couple alone.\n- **Events inside the session.** A proposal plus portraits, a session that includes a picnic setup or a boat: the event eats thirty minutes before photography starts.\n- **Small children, counterintuitively.** Not because you shoot for two hours — because the second hour buys breaks. Forty-five minutes of shooting spread over ninety of walking, snacking and playing beats sixty tense minutes.\n\n## The honest middle: ninety minutes\n\nMany photographers in Spain offer 90 minutes, and for couples it is often the truthful answer: one area worked properly at golden hour, a slow transfer on foot, a second backdrop for the last light. If you are choosing between \"one hour feels tight\" and \"two hours feels like a production\", ask whether a 90-minute option exists before deciding.\n\n## What doesn't change with length\n\nThe photographer's preparation, the editing quality per photo and the delivery pipeline are the same at any duration — which is why two hours never costs double one hour, and why the shortest session is the most expensive per minute. Judge the [package prices](/photoshoots/couples) against what you want on the wall, not against the per-minute maths.\n\n## The decision in one line\n\nOne area, one outfit, up to four people: **one hour**. Two worlds, five-plus people, an event inside, or toddlers: **two**. In between, with golden light involved: **look for ninety**. What moves the price beyond duration — city, day of week, extras — is covered in [what a photoshoot in Spain costs](/blog/what-a-photoshoot-in-spain-costs).\n"
